  "prediction": "The Department of Defense (DOD) establishes foreign currency budget rates to determine its funding needs for overseas expenditures. These rates are used to calculate gains or losses due to foreign currency fluctuations. The report examines DOD's use of foreign currency budget rates and its management of the Foreign Currency Fluctuations Defense (FCFD) account.\n\nDOD has revised its foreign currency budget rates since 2009 to more closely align with market rates. The revisions have decreased DOD's projected O&M and MILPERS funding needs and potential gains and losses due to foreign currency fluctuations. However, DOD has not consistently selected the most cost-effective foreign currency rates for disbursements, resulting in potential cost savings opportunities.\n\nThe report also found that DOD has not effectively managed the FCFD account balance based on projected gains or losses. The account balance has been maintained at the maximum level allowed by statute, which may be higher than necessary to cover losses. DOD has not analyzed realized and projected losses to determine the necessary size of the FCFD account balance.\n\nThe report makes four recommendations to DOD:\n\n1. Conduct a review of foreign currency rates used at disbursement to determine cost-saving opportunities.\n2. Analyze realized and projected losses to determine the necessary size of the FCFD account balance.\n3. Revise the Financial Management Regulation to include guidance on ensuring complete and accurate data in foreign currency reports.\n4. Develop a plan to modify the General Fund Enterprise Business System to accurately record disbursements.\n\nDOD partially concurred with the second recommendation, citing the difficulty in forecasting foreign currency rates due to market volatility. However, the report notes that DOD has calculated actual and projected losses due to foreign currency fluctuations and uses those projections as the basis for transfers out of the FCFD account.\n\nThe report concludes that DOD can improve its management of foreign currency gains and losses by analyzing realized and projected losses, selecting cost-effective foreign currency rates, and ensuring complete and accurate data in financial reports.",
